mpaign's outcome is that child's first evidence.\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 3 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Transformation (confidence: 0.92)\n   Why this fits: The core of the task is a 'data reshape' (Integer to UUID) and a multi-step migration process. The Transformation behavioral subtree is the best fit for the primary operation, especially with its emphasis on deterministic shapes and the use of :code for high-fidelity changes. Its strengths in :map-each are highly relevant for the 'backfill in batches' requirement.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Transformation is mechanical reshape of data between deterministic shapes. ALWAYS use :code (not :llm) for deterministic transformations — preserves fidelity, costs nothing, runs in ms; :llm for transformation produces non-deterministic output. For per-item independent transformations, :map-each isolates per-item failures. ALWAYS declare :output-schemas on transformation output and :input-schemas on downstream :reads so shape mismatch fails at the boundary, not deep in the consumer. Composes into etl-pipeline (sequential transform chain), parallel-sum (map-reduce transformation), sequential-pipeline shells, and map-reduce shells. Avoid for transformations that need judgment (semantic rephrasing / style shift — use :llm) or for unstructured input (extract first).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given input data + a target shape spec, emit the input restructured to the target shape\n  - natural fit anywhere a tree needs to change data shape without semantic interpretation\n  - outputs are typically a single restructured value or a vector of per-item transformations\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** implement transformations in :code (NOT :llm) when the mapping is deterministic — preserves byte-for-byte fidelity, costs zero tokens, runs in milliseconds (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the transformation rule can be expressed as a pure function over the input\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:input] :writes [:output] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:output (mapv (fn [r] (select-keys r [:a :b])) (:input inputs))})}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for per-item independent transformations over a vector, use :map-each rather than a single batch :code call when the per-item work is non-trivial — supports per-item failure isolation (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: items can fail independently AND partial success has value\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:map-each {:items :records :as :record :writes [:transformed]} [:code {:reads [:record] :writes [:transformed] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:transformed (assoc (:record inputs) :stamp \"x\")})}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** using :llm for a deterministic transformation (CSV → JSON, snake_case → camelCase) produces non-deterministic output AND wastes tokens (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the transformation rule is fully specifiable\n    - Recommended fix: always :code for deterministic transformations; reserve :llm for transformations that need judgment (semantic-rephrasing / style-shift / domain-translation)\n\n  - **Failure mode:** transformations without :output-schemas on the downstream :reads break silently when an upstream change shifts the output shape — the consumer gets garbage instead of an error (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the transformation feeds another stage that binds on specific fields\n    - Recommended fix: declare :output-schemas on the transformation output AND :input-schemas on the downstream :reads — shape mismatch fails loudly at the boundary, not deep in the consumer\n\nRepresenta